.TH ASIN 3 "May 2026" "Substrate" "Library Functions Manual"
.SH NAME
asin, asinf, asinl, acos, acosf, acosl, atan, atanf, atanl, atan2, atan2f, atan2l \- inverse trigonometric functions
.SH LIBRARY
Math library (libm, \-lm)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B #include <math.h>
.PP
.BI "double asin(double " x );
.br
.BI "float asinf(float " x );
.br
.BI "long double asinl(long double " x );
.PP
.BI "double acos(double " x );
.br
.BI "float acosf(float " x );
.br
.BI "long double acosl(long double " x );
.PP
.BI "double atan(double " x );
.br
.BI "float atanf(float " x );
.br
.BI "long double atanl(long double " x );
.PP
.BI "double atan2(double " y ", double " x );
.br
.BI "float atan2f(float " y ", float " x );
.br
.BI "long double atan2l(long double " y ", long double " x );
.SH DESCRIPTION
The
.BR asin ()
function returns the principal value of the arc sine of
.IR x ,
expressed in radians.  The argument must lie in the closed interval
.BR [\-1,\ +1] ,
and the result lies in
.BR [\-\(*p/2,\ +\(*p/2] .
.PP
The
.BR acos ()
function returns the principal value of the arc cosine of
.IR x ,
expressed in radians.  The argument must lie in the closed interval
.BR [\-1,\ +1] ,
and the result lies in
.BR [0,\ \(*p] .
.PP
The
.BR atan ()
function returns the principal value of the arc tangent of
.IR x ,
expressed in radians.  The argument may be any finite real number, and
the result lies in the open interval
.BR (\-\(*p/2,\ +\(*p/2) .
.PP
The
.BR atan2 ()
function returns the principal value of the arc tangent of
.IR y / x ,
using the signs of both arguments to determine the quadrant of the
result.  A positive result represents a counterclockwise angle from the
positive
.I x
axis; a negative result represents a clockwise angle.  The result lies
in the half-open interval
.BR (\-\(*p,\ +\(*p] .
.PP
The
.BR asinf (),
.BR acosf (),
.BR atanf (),
and
.BR atan2f ()
variants take and return
.B float
values.  The
.BR asinl (),
.BR acosl (),
.BR atanl (),
and
.BR atan2l ()
variants take and return
.B "long double"
values.
.SH RETURN VALUE
On success, these functions return the inverse trigonometric value of
their argument(s) as described above.
.PP
If
.I x
is a NaN, all functions return a NaN.  For
.BR atan2 (),
if either argument is a NaN, a NaN is returned.
.PP
For
.BR asin ()
and
.BR acos (),
if the absolute value of
.I x
exceeds 1, a domain error occurs and the functions return NaN.
.PP
For
.BR atan (),
if
.I x
is positive infinity,
.IR +\(*p/2
is returned; if
.I x
is negative infinity,
.IR \-\(*p/2
is returned.
.PP
For
.BR atan2 (),
the special cases follow C99 Annex F.10.1.4.  In particular:
.IP \(bu 2
.BI atan2( \(+-0 ,\  \-0 )
returns
.IR \(+-\(*p .
.IP \(bu 2
.BI atan2( \(+-0 ,\  +0 )
returns
.IR \(+-0 .
.IP \(bu 2
.BI atan2( \(+-0 ,\  x )
returns
.I \(+-\(*p
for
.I x
< 0, and
.I \(+-0
for
.I x
> 0.
.IP \(bu 2
.BI atan2( y ,\  \(+-0 )
returns
.I \-\(*p/2
for
.I y
< 0, and
.I +\(*p/2
for
.I y
> 0.
.IP \(bu 2
.BI atan2( \(+-y ,\  \-\[if] )
returns
.IR \(+-\(*p ,
and
.BI atan2( \(+-y ,\  +\[if] )
returns
.IR \(+-0 ,
for finite positive
.IR y .
.IP \(bu 2
.BI atan2( \(+-\[if] ,\  x )
returns
.I \(+-\(*p/2
for finite
.IR x .
.IP \(bu 2
.BI atan2( \(+-\[if] ,\  \-\[if] )
returns
.IR \(+-3\(*p/4 .
.IP \(bu 2
.BI atan2( \(+-\[if] ,\  +\[if] )
returns
.IR \(+-\(*p/4 .
.SH ERRORS
See
.BR math_error (7)
for information on how to determine whether an error has occurred when
calling these functions.
.PP
The following errors can occur:
.TP
Domain error: \fIx\fR is outside [\-1, +1] (\fBasin\fR, \fBacos\fR)
An invalid floating-point exception
.RB ( FE_INVALID )
is raised.  If
.I "math_errhandling & MATH_ERRNO"
is non-zero,
.I errno
is set to
.BR EDOM .
.PP
.BR atan ()
and
.BR atan2 ()
do not raise domain errors for any combination of finite or infinite
arguments; their results for the boundary cases are defined as listed
in RETURN VALUE.
.SH ATTRIBUTES
For an explanation of the terms used in this section, see
.BR attributes (7).
.TS
allbox;
lb lb lb
l l l.
Interface	Attribute	Value
T{
.BR asin (),
.BR asinf (),
.BR asinl (),
.BR acos (),
.BR acosf (),
.BR acosl (),
.BR atan (),
.BR atanf (),
.BR atanl (),
.BR atan2 (),
.BR atan2f (),
.BR atan2l ()
T}	Thread safety	MT-Safe
.TE
.SH STANDARDS
.BR asin (),
.BR acos (),
.BR atan (),
.BR atan2 (),
and their
.BR f / l
variants conform to C99 and POSIX.1-2008.  The handling of special
arguments for
.BR atan2 ()
follows C99 Annex F.10.1.4 (IEC 60559).
.SH EXAMPLES
The following program computes
.IR asin(1.0) ,
which equals \(*p/2:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
#include <math.h>
#include <stdio.h>

int
main(void)
{
    double x = 1.0;

    printf("asin(%.6f) = %.6f\en", x, asin(x));
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
The next example uses
.BR atan2 ()
to recover the angle of the vector
.RI ( 1 ,\  1 ) ,
which equals \(*p/4 (45\(de):
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
#include <math.h>
#include <stdio.h>

int
main(void)
{
    double y = 1.0, x = 1.0;
    double theta = atan2(y, x);

    printf("atan2(%.1f, %.1f) = %.6f rad (%.2f deg)\en",
           y, x, theta, theta * 180.0 / M_PI);
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
The following program triggers a domain error by passing an out-of-range
argument to
.BR asin ():
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
#include <errno.h>
#include <math.h>
#include <stdio.h>
#include <string.h>

int
main(void)
{
    double r;

    errno = 0;
    r = asin(2.0);
    if (errno != 0)
        printf("asin(2.0) = %f, errno = %d (%s)\en",
               r, errno, strerror(errno));
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
